The summands of the embedding i_∞ increase with the index: for each x in D_∞ and each m, `iInfTerm_le_succ` says the m-th summand at x lies below the (m+1)-st summand at x. `iInfTerm_monotone` is that fact for the whole sequence.

The pointwise order reduces the successor comparison to values at an arbitrary z. The successor formula for the inverse-limit embedding rewrites the lower value one level up, and monotonicity of the embedding at that level reduces the claim to an inequality of arguments. Inverse-limit coherence replaces the (m+1)-st component of x by the retraction of the next component. The successor formula for the tower retraction then moves the level-m inclusion inside that component. The projection inequality drops the inclusion-retraction pair, and monotonicity of the next component, fed by the inequality between an embedded component and the next projection, completes the comparison. A sequence indexed by the natural numbers is monotone once it increases from each index to the next.
